Days occupy cells w through w+n-1 when the month is laid out row by row from Monday. The number of rows is therefore ceil((w+n)/7), which equals (w+n+6)//7.

The postcondition can avoid division by characterising r directly as the smallest row count that fits all cells:
- 7*r >= w+n, so r rows hold every cell;
- 7*(r-1) < w+n, so one fewer row would not be enough.

Together these fix r uniquely.

```python
def pre(w, n):
    return And(w >= 0, w <= 6, n >= 28, n <= 31)

def post(w, n, r):
    return And(7 * r >= w + n, 7 * (r - 1) < w + n)
```
